<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aulic War Council</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1556">1556</a>–<a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Hofkriegsrat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aulic War Council, or Hofkriegsrat, was the central military administration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg Monarchy</a>, established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1556. It oversaw the army, fortresses, supply and the Military Frontier against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and for long periods also conducted relations with the Ottoman Empire. Often criticised for interfering in the operations of field commanders, it was replaced by a Ministry of War in 1848.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aulic-war-council.jpg" alt="Aulic War Council" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hofkriegsratsgeb%C3%A4ude_Am_Hof_Wien_1775.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Emperor Maximilian I had already summoned permanent councils of war about 1500, and on 25 February 1531 Ferdinand I ordered from Linz an independent war council of four councillors. The Permanent War Council founded under Ferdinand on 17 November 1556 was a body of five generals and senior civil servants. It decided on fortress building, equipment, pay and the purchase of supplies, planned and conducted wars, and ran the civil and military administration of the border region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. On 31 December 1556 all military authorities were ordered to submit to it, and the title Hofkriegsrat was first used in 1564.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Once a standing Imperial Army existed in the 17th century, the council managed it as the central military agency and chancery, provided a staff for the emperor and directed the field armies. Its presidents included Raimondo Montecuccoli and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy">Prince Eugene of Savoy</a>. Every general except the generalissimo had to seek its authorisation for strategic decisions, a rule that kept action coordinated but proved a handicap against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. Joseph II gave it supreme authority over every branch of military administration.</p><p>In 1799 its instructions, which called for trench warfare instead of lightning warfare, held up Field Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-suvorov">Alexander Suvorov</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Archduke Charles, made president by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> in 1801, divided it into military, judicial and administrative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> it was one of the four parts of the State Council. Its business moved slowly through argument and circulating papers, the officer presidents clashed with civilian section heads, and the general staff remained a subordinate section of it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In its last years the council investigated units of doubtful loyalty: in 1833 it ruled that soldiers belonging to Mazzini's Young Italy were guilty of high treason, and in the 1840s it judged that the South Slav Grenzer would obey orders. From 1 June 1848 it became the Austrian Ministry of War, which under the Austro-Hungarian Compromise of 1867 was one of the three common ministries. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-tolstoy">Tolstoy</a>'s War and Peace the old Prince Bolkonski mocks it as the Hof-kriegs-wurst-schnapps-rat.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hofkriegsrat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After surviving the siege of Vienna in 1529, the Habsburgs built the Military Frontier, a fortified belt along the Croatian and Hungarian border settled by soldier-farmers, many of them Serb and Vlach refugees, and directed after 1556 by the <b>Aulic War Council</b> (Hofkriegsrat) in Vienna.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Military</a></li></ul></section>
